facts:
The **angle at the centre** is formed by joining both endpoints of the chord to the centre $O$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above] at (O) {$O$};
\coordinate (A) at (200:2);
\coordinate (B) at (340:2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (O) -- (A);
\draw (O) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$\angle AOB$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=2.6] {angle = A--O--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The **angle at the circumference** is formed by joining both endpoints of the chord to any other point on the circle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below right] at (O) {$O$};
\coordinate (A) at (200:2);
\coordinate (B) at (340:2);
\coordinate (C) at (90:2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above] at (C) {$C$};
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$\angle ACB$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=2.8] {angle = A--C--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An angle **stands on** the arc between its arms that does not contain its own vertex.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw[red, thick] (200:2) arc[start angle=200, end angle=340, radius=2];
\coordinate (A) at (200:2);
\coordinate (B) at (340:2);
\coordinate (C) at (90:2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above] at (C) {$C$};
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (A);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$\angle ACB$", angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=2.8] {angle = A--C--B};
\node[below] at (0,-2.15) {minor arc $AB$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

